WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:02.160
- [Narrator] Legal Eagle
is coming in loud and clear

00:00:02.160 --> 00:00:04.330
thanks to Ting Mobile.

00:00:04.330 --> 00:00:05.500
(upbeat music)

00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:07.800
- What about human rights law?

00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:08.741
- Oh no.

00:00:08.741 --> 00:00:10.050
♪ No money, no, no money ♪

00:00:10.050 --> 00:00:11.040
- Oh no!

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:12.968
- Environmental law?

00:00:12.968 --> 00:00:15.950
♪ No money, even less money ♪

00:00:15.950 --> 00:00:17.670
- Immigration law?

00:00:17.670 --> 00:00:20.360
♪ No money, plus it's a bummer ♪

00:00:20.360 --> 00:00:21.870
(laughs)

00:00:21.870 --> 00:00:24.840
- That hurts my heart
because it's so true.

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:28.060
(playful music)

00:00:28.060 --> 00:00:30.550
Hey legal eagles, it's time
to think like a lawyer.

00:00:30.550 --> 00:00:32.910
Today we are covering the long-requested

00:00:32.910 --> 00:00:35.970
Crazy Ex-Girlfriend, and
specifically a music video

00:00:35.970 --> 00:00:37.840
that shows up in season four

00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:39.796
called "Don't Be a Lawyer."

00:00:39.796 --> 00:00:41.490
♪ Don't be a lawyer ♪

00:00:41.490 --> 00:00:44.220
- I remember really liking
Crazy Ex-Girlfriend,

00:00:44.220 --> 00:00:46.530
I think I saw a couple of
the episodes from season one.

00:00:46.530 --> 00:00:48.240
They clearly did their research about

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:49.540
what it's like to be an attorney,

00:00:49.540 --> 00:00:51.320
and the musical numbers
were really good too.

00:00:51.320 --> 00:00:53.670
If memory serves, I think
this is about a woman

00:00:53.670 --> 00:00:56.160
who is a partner in a Boston law firm

00:00:56.160 --> 00:00:58.150
or she's just about to become partner

00:00:58.150 --> 00:01:00.020
and she decides to give that up,

00:01:00.020 --> 00:01:03.230
follow an ex-boyfriend or
someone she wanted to be

00:01:03.230 --> 00:01:05.330
her boyfriend out to California,

00:01:05.330 --> 00:01:06.780
but not any place in California,

00:01:06.780 --> 00:01:09.320
specifically to Riverside, California,

00:01:09.320 --> 00:01:11.300
or to some other place
in the Inland Empire,

00:01:11.300 --> 00:01:13.620
which I've actually practiced there,

00:01:13.620 --> 00:01:17.080
and it's not my favorite
place in the world.

00:01:17.080 --> 00:01:18.600
Come at me, West Covina.

00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:20.770
Oh boy, I'm gonna get
some hate for that one.

00:01:20.770 --> 00:01:23.080
As always, be sure to comment
in the form of an objection

00:01:23.080 --> 00:01:25.210
which I will either sustain or overrule,

00:01:25.210 --> 00:01:26.710
and stick around until
the end of the video

00:01:26.710 --> 00:01:29.310
where I give Crazy Ex-Girlfriend
"Don't Be a Lawyer"

00:01:29.310 --> 00:01:31.260
a grade for legal realism.

00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:34.683
So without further ado, let's
dig in to Crazy Ex-Girlfriend.

00:01:35.570 --> 00:01:37.440
- I'm majoring in pre-law myself.

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:40.978
- Oh no, no, no, bad idea, trust me.

00:01:40.978 --> 00:01:43.207
Being a lawyer is the worst.

00:01:43.207 --> 00:01:44.690
- (chuckles) Rude.

00:01:44.690 --> 00:01:47.072
- Sit back and let me
tell you my tale of woe.

00:01:47.072 --> 00:01:49.655
(upbeat music)

00:01:54.106 --> 00:01:56.213
♪ 20 years old, pretty smart kid ♪

00:01:56.213 --> 00:01:58.446
♪ Didn't know what I wanted to do ♪

00:01:58.446 --> 00:01:59.739
♪ So I took the LSAT ♪

00:01:59.739 --> 00:02:00.915
♪ And then just like that ♪

00:02:00.915 --> 00:02:03.431
♪ Got accepted at Glendale U ♪

00:02:03.431 --> 00:02:07.470
- Okay, right off the bat,
this is definitely accurate

00:02:07.470 --> 00:02:09.730
to a lot of people that
want to go to law school.

00:02:09.730 --> 00:02:10.950
First of all, the test that you take

00:02:10.950 --> 00:02:12.340
to get into law school is called the LSAT,

00:02:12.340 --> 00:02:14.640
you probably know that,
but more importantly,

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:16.610
there's so many people who aren't sure

00:02:16.610 --> 00:02:17.980
what they want to do for a living,

00:02:17.980 --> 00:02:19.980
and so they delay going
into the real world

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:22.590
by taking the LSAT and
going to law school,

00:02:22.590 --> 00:02:25.110
and invariably, these
are the people that have

00:02:25.110 --> 00:02:27.200
the worst experiences in law school,

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:30.480
because law school is a
whole bucket of stress,

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:33.260
and if you aren't sure, if
you aren't absolutely positive

00:02:33.260 --> 00:02:35.960
you want to be a lawyer,
don't go to law school,

00:02:35.960 --> 00:02:36.860
it's not worth it.

00:02:36.860 --> 00:02:39.130
All of the advantages of
law school can be achieved

00:02:39.130 --> 00:02:40.980
in other places for a lot less money

00:02:40.980 --> 00:02:42.420
under a lot less stress.

00:02:42.420 --> 00:02:44.510
Frankly, you're probably
better off just getting a loan

00:02:44.510 --> 00:02:47.290
for $300,000 and then lighting it on fire

00:02:47.290 --> 00:02:48.400
and posting it to Instagram,

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:50.210
because that's pretty
much what you're doing

00:02:50.210 --> 00:02:51.390
if you're going to law school

00:02:51.390 --> 00:02:53.617
and you're not really sure
you want to be a lawyer.

00:02:53.617 --> 00:02:56.232
♪ Everyone said it was a real safe bet ♪

00:02:56.232 --> 00:02:58.431
♪ A prestigious and lucrative vocation ♪

00:02:58.431 --> 00:02:59.604
♪ So I set out on a journey ♪

00:02:59.604 --> 00:03:00.861
♪ To become an attorney ♪

00:03:00.861 --> 00:03:03.660
♪ Without a moment's hesitation ♪

00:03:03.660 --> 00:03:06.590
- Yeah okay, so this is another
big myth about law school.

00:03:06.590 --> 00:03:08.200
People think that it's prestigious

00:03:08.200 --> 00:03:10.290
and that you're guaranteed
to get a good job.

00:03:10.290 --> 00:03:13.420
The thing is that the
really well-paying jobs

00:03:13.420 --> 00:03:16.140
out of law school really are only achieved

00:03:16.140 --> 00:03:18.500
by maybe the top 10% of law graduates,

00:03:18.500 --> 00:03:20.370
and if you go to a really good law school,

00:03:20.370 --> 00:03:22.270
like say Harvard or Yale or something,

00:03:22.270 --> 00:03:24.680
maybe the top 50% of those graduates

00:03:24.680 --> 00:03:26.690
get the really prestigious
and well-paying jobs.

00:03:26.690 --> 00:03:28.710
If you're going to a lower-ranked school,

00:03:28.710 --> 00:03:31.430
the chances are less that
you'll get those jobs,

00:03:31.430 --> 00:03:34.860
and you might be making just
barely over the median salary.

00:03:34.860 --> 00:03:36.990
The median salary for law graduates

00:03:36.990 --> 00:03:39.000
is about $70,000, which granted,

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:41.410
is a lot more than the
median salary in the US,

00:03:41.410 --> 00:03:43.740
which is about $55,000, but still,

00:03:43.740 --> 00:03:46.330
it costs you hundreds of
thousands of dollars to get that,

00:03:46.330 --> 00:03:48.020
and that doesn't go very
far if you're living

00:03:48.020 --> 00:03:50.337
in a major city like
New York or Los Angeles.

00:03:50.337 --> 00:03:54.050
♪ But here's some free advice I'm giving ♪

00:03:54.050 --> 00:03:55.020
- Not legal advice.

00:03:55.020 --> 00:03:59.659
♪ When it comes to deciding
what to do for a living ♪

00:03:59.659 --> 00:04:02.140
♪ Don't be a lawyer ♪
(laughs)

00:04:02.140 --> 00:04:03.520
- So look, like I said,

00:04:03.520 --> 00:04:05.520
if you aren't sure that
you want to be a lawyer,

00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:07.350
you should definitely not be a lawyer,

00:04:07.350 --> 00:04:09.300
and even if you are sure
that you want to be a lawyer,

00:04:09.300 --> 00:04:11.580
sometimes it's not the
best decision you can make,

00:04:11.580 --> 00:04:13.217
and frankly, there are
so many people that say

00:04:13.217 --> 00:04:15.140
"oh, I'm really good at arguing,"

00:04:15.140 --> 00:04:16.637
or "I'm really good on my feet,

00:04:16.637 --> 00:04:18.370
"so therefore I should be an attorney."

00:04:18.370 --> 00:04:19.740
Lawyers in real life actually

00:04:19.740 --> 00:04:22.530
don't do that kind of
stuff all that often.

00:04:22.530 --> 00:04:23.940
Certainly if you're a trial lawyer,

00:04:23.940 --> 00:04:25.830
you'll probably be in court more often

00:04:25.830 --> 00:04:29.070
than transactional
attorneys, but even then,

00:04:29.070 --> 00:04:30.750
almost all of what attorneys do

00:04:30.750 --> 00:04:33.140
is legal research and legal writing.

00:04:33.140 --> 00:04:36.510
You are stuck in a cubicle
or an office and you're just

00:04:36.510 --> 00:04:38.520
writing in front of a
computer screen all day.

00:04:38.520 --> 00:04:40.690
So it's definitely not as glamorous

00:04:40.690 --> 00:04:42.800
as it's often made out to be.

00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:45.153
♪ Don't be a lawyer ♪

00:04:45.153 --> 00:04:47.568
♪ Don't do it, quickest
way to ruin your life ♪

00:04:47.568 --> 00:04:49.382
♪ Don't be a lawyer ♪

00:04:49.382 --> 00:04:53.191
♪ Not worth it, I'll
leave you dead inside ♪

00:04:53.191 --> 00:04:56.190
- Ah, these guys have some
pretty interesting suits,

00:04:56.190 --> 00:04:58.870
if only there was some
sort of custom suit-maker

00:04:58.870 --> 00:05:01.960
that could make really nice
custom slim-fit suits for them

00:05:01.960 --> 00:05:04.300
instead of these weird pink and burgundy

00:05:04.300 --> 00:05:05.630
things that they're wearing.

00:05:05.630 --> 00:05:06.617
Indochino.

00:05:06.617 --> 00:05:08.829
♪ The job is inherently crappy ♪

00:05:08.829 --> 00:05:11.117
♪ That's why you've never
met a lawyer who's happy ♪

00:05:11.117 --> 00:05:15.516
♪ It's a guaranteed soul destroyer ♪

00:05:15.516 --> 00:05:17.680
♪ Don't be a lawyer ♪

00:05:17.680 --> 00:05:19.410
- Oh wait a minute, so
the blackboard here,

00:05:19.410 --> 00:05:21.499
this is, it's a Bar exam.

00:05:21.499 --> 00:05:23.810
All of these students
are taking the Bar exam.

00:05:23.810 --> 00:05:26.210
You can tell because in the
bottom right hand corner,

00:05:26.210 --> 00:05:27.440
it mentions the MBE.

00:05:27.440 --> 00:05:29.230
The MBE is the Multistate Bar Exam,

00:05:29.230 --> 00:05:31.420
it's sort of universal Bar exam,

00:05:31.420 --> 00:05:33.720
at least for the multiple
choice questions.

00:05:33.720 --> 00:05:35.210
Every state has a different Bar exam,

00:05:35.210 --> 00:05:37.890
so no lawyer can practice
in every single state,

00:05:37.890 --> 00:05:40.220
you'd have to take every
single state's Bar exam,

00:05:40.220 --> 00:05:42.710
but the MBE allows states to sort of reuse

00:05:42.710 --> 00:05:45.140
multiple choice questions
in a single year,

00:05:45.140 --> 00:05:48.270
and funny story, when I
was taking the Bar exam,

00:05:48.270 --> 00:05:49.780
on the first day, and this, remember,

00:05:49.780 --> 00:05:51.620
was in Los Angeles, California,

00:05:51.620 --> 00:05:55.500
there was an earthquake during
the first session of the exam

00:05:55.500 --> 00:05:57.330
and you could tell who was from California

00:05:57.330 --> 00:05:59.070
and who wasn't from California

00:05:59.070 --> 00:06:01.420
because the ones who
were not from California

00:06:01.420 --> 00:06:02.680
were hiding under their desk,

00:06:02.680 --> 00:06:04.530
and then the Californians just went on

00:06:04.530 --> 00:06:05.590
continuing with their test

00:06:05.590 --> 00:06:08.950
while the out-of-state folks
were cowering for their lives.

00:06:08.950 --> 00:06:10.443
That's why I passed.

00:06:10.443 --> 00:06:13.693
(upbeat hip-hop music)

00:06:15.180 --> 00:06:18.110
Okay, despite what you may
want, I am absolutely not

00:06:18.110 --> 00:06:21.190
even going to try to
attempt to learn this dance.

00:06:21.190 --> 00:06:23.010
I will say though that
in doing some research

00:06:23.010 --> 00:06:25.740
for this video, I did see
that another legal YouTuber

00:06:25.740 --> 00:06:27.995
had both covered this
video and impressively,

00:06:27.995 --> 00:06:29.860
learned this dance.

00:06:29.860 --> 00:06:31.479
♪ Don't be a lawyer ♪

00:06:31.479 --> 00:06:34.480
♪ Don't do it, the quickest
way to ruin your life ♪

00:06:34.480 --> 00:06:35.740
- Her name is Even Cornwell,

00:06:35.740 --> 00:06:39.020
she is a law student from
the UK, though I think now

00:06:39.020 --> 00:06:41.240
she's probably an actual
practicing attorney.

00:06:41.240 --> 00:06:42.930
I'll put a link to her
video in the description,

00:06:42.930 --> 00:06:45.070
definitely check her out and
give her some subscribers,

00:06:45.070 --> 00:06:47.130
and by the way, I was
checking my analytics,

00:06:47.130 --> 00:06:49.600
and only 30% of the people
that watch my videos

00:06:49.600 --> 00:06:51.170
are actually subscribed to my channel,

00:06:51.170 --> 00:06:55.300
so please, seriously,
subscribe, what are you doing?

00:06:55.300 --> 00:06:57.295
♪ Law school debt, daily regret ♪

00:06:57.295 --> 00:06:59.712
♪ Is that what you dreamed of as a kid ♪

00:06:59.712 --> 00:07:02.128
♪ Or did you hope one day
that you'd find a way ♪

00:07:02.128 --> 00:07:03.687
♪ To spend four years workin' on a ♪

00:07:03.687 --> 00:07:06.315
♪ Pharmaceutical company's
merger with another ♪

00:07:06.315 --> 00:07:10.642
♪ Pharmaceutical company ♪
(laughing)

00:07:10.642 --> 00:07:12.750
- (groans) Oh, that's so true.

00:07:12.750 --> 00:07:15.160
Well, it's partially true, so like I said,

00:07:15.160 --> 00:07:16.510
when you're a young attorney,

00:07:16.510 --> 00:07:18.550
you get staffed on these giant cases,

00:07:18.550 --> 00:07:21.320
especially if you go into
big law, which is what I did.

00:07:21.320 --> 00:07:23.690
The only companies and
individuals that can afford

00:07:23.690 --> 00:07:26.330
these ridiculous rates of like
a thousand dollars an hour

00:07:26.330 --> 00:07:29.190
are huge companies, Fortune 500 companies,

00:07:29.190 --> 00:07:30.960
and so odds are you're gonna get staffed

00:07:30.960 --> 00:07:33.730
on something like a
pharmaceutical company's merger

00:07:33.730 --> 00:07:35.650
with another pharmaceutical company.

00:07:35.650 --> 00:07:38.380
Though in fairness, it's really
only transactional attorneys

00:07:38.380 --> 00:07:40.570
that are going to be stuck
on something like that.

00:07:40.570 --> 00:07:42.330
If you're a litigator,
odds are you'll get stuck

00:07:42.330 --> 00:07:44.350
on something like the
pharmaceutical company's

00:07:44.350 --> 00:07:47.300
bankruptcy pan or the
pharmaceutical company's

00:07:47.300 --> 00:07:49.590
law suit against another
pharmaceutical company

00:07:49.590 --> 00:07:53.970
for a possible infringement
of some pharmaceutical patent.

00:07:53.970 --> 00:07:55.990
And generally speaking,
there are two main types

00:07:55.990 --> 00:07:58.110
of attorneys, there are
the transactional attorneys

00:07:58.110 --> 00:08:01.610
that are gonna work on the
contracts and the business plans

00:08:01.610 --> 00:08:03.490
and mergers and acquisitions,

00:08:03.490 --> 00:08:05.240
and then there are the
people that you call in

00:08:05.240 --> 00:08:07.120
when something goes wrong to litigate,

00:08:07.120 --> 00:08:08.610
the litigators and trial lawyers,

00:08:08.610 --> 00:08:11.030
the let's call them real lawyers.

00:08:11.030 --> 00:08:12.340
No, I don't want you to think that

00:08:12.340 --> 00:08:14.680
transactional attorneys
aren't real lawyers,

00:08:14.680 --> 00:08:16.030
I mean, they're not real lawyers,

00:08:16.030 --> 00:08:18.160
but I definitely don't
want you to think that.

00:08:18.160 --> 00:08:22.010
Oh boy, I am gonna get some
very well-worded angry emails

00:08:22.010 --> 00:08:24.410
from a bunch of transactional attorneys.

00:08:24.410 --> 00:08:26.275
But what are they gonna do, sue me?

00:08:26.275 --> 00:08:27.628
(scoffs) They can't.

00:08:27.628 --> 00:08:30.024
♪ Your only expertise is runnin' up fees ♪

00:08:30.024 --> 00:08:32.602
♪ Speakin' legalese like (bleep) ♪

00:08:32.602 --> 00:08:34.840
(laughs)

00:08:34.840 --> 00:08:37.590
- Big myth, actually we
try to avoid using legalese

00:08:37.590 --> 00:08:39.630
because it's really not persuasive.

00:08:39.630 --> 00:08:42.350
Whether it's to a layperson
like a member of a jury

00:08:42.350 --> 00:08:44.740
of the judge, we try to avoid any legalese

00:08:44.740 --> 00:08:45.960
if we can possibly avoid it,

00:08:45.960 --> 00:08:47.510
so it's really mainly the law students

00:08:47.510 --> 00:08:50.950
that use legalese in
completely improper situations.

00:08:50.950 --> 00:08:53.189
♪ But it's not too late
to avoid this fate ♪

00:08:53.189 --> 00:08:55.806
♪ Find any other job to pick ♪

00:08:55.806 --> 00:09:00.511
♪ Sure, your parents might
think you're a failure ♪

00:09:00.511 --> 00:09:02.408
♪ But no one's ever said ♪

00:09:02.408 --> 00:09:05.550
♪ "First let's kill all the tailors" ♪

00:09:05.550 --> 00:09:07.170
- Why would anyone want to kill someone

00:09:07.170 --> 00:09:10.720
that makes such affordable,
high-quality custom-made suits?

00:09:10.720 --> 00:09:11.870
Indochino.

00:09:11.870 --> 00:09:14.030
And this kind of reminds
me of a famous lawyer joke

00:09:14.030 --> 00:09:16.470
where a person is talking to the devil,

00:09:16.470 --> 00:09:19.037
the they say "For my
first wish, I wish that

00:09:19.037 --> 00:09:21.100
"all the lawyers were dead or disappeared"

00:09:21.100 --> 00:09:23.580
and the devil grants it and
all the lawyers are gone,

00:09:23.580 --> 00:09:25.080
and then they make a second wish,

00:09:25.080 --> 00:09:28.378
and the devil reneges on the agreement,

00:09:28.378 --> 00:09:31.117
and he says "what are
you gonna do, sue me?

00:09:31.117 --> 00:09:32.380
"All the lawyers are gone!"

00:09:32.380 --> 00:09:34.470
I know you all wish we would disappear,

00:09:34.470 --> 00:09:38.701
but it's in everyone's best
interest if we don't, I'm sorry.

00:09:38.701 --> 00:09:40.776
♪ Don't be a lawyer ♪

00:09:40.776 --> 00:09:43.315
♪ I'm serious, it really, really sucks ♪

00:09:43.315 --> 00:09:45.316
♪ Don't be a lawyer ♪

00:09:45.316 --> 00:09:48.490
♪ No one you work with
looks like Ally McBeal ♪

00:09:48.490 --> 00:09:50.290
(laughing)

00:09:50.290 --> 00:09:51.900
- Oh man, I don't know if you guys

00:09:51.900 --> 00:09:53.710
are familiar with Ally McBeal.

00:09:53.710 --> 00:09:56.570
I definitely grew up watching Ally McBeal,

00:09:56.570 --> 00:09:57.710
and god, I love it.

00:09:57.710 --> 00:10:01.600
That, obviously I loved Law
&amp; Order, probably the best,

00:10:01.600 --> 00:10:03.090
and Law &amp; Order is one of the reasons

00:10:03.090 --> 00:10:06.110
that I became a lawyer, it
was amazing, but frankly,

00:10:06.110 --> 00:10:08.424
Ally McBeal made it seem
like a whole lot more fun.

00:10:08.424 --> 00:10:11.820
♪ I can't stop this feeling ♪

00:10:11.820 --> 00:10:13.390
- I should probably do
a real lawyer reacts

00:10:13.390 --> 00:10:14.570
to Ally McBeal at some point.

00:10:14.570 --> 00:10:16.197
Let me know if I should do that.

00:10:16.197 --> 00:10:18.469
♪ There are so many other professions ♪

00:10:18.469 --> 00:10:20.880
♪ That don't turn you into Jeff Sessions ♪

00:10:20.880 --> 00:10:21.713
(laughs)

00:10:21.713 --> 00:10:24.780
- Look, Jeff Sessions is barely a lawyer.

00:10:24.780 --> 00:10:27.460
Don't lump him into everyone else

00:10:27.460 --> 00:10:29.700
that happens to practice
in the legal field.

00:10:29.700 --> 00:10:30.920
We may not look like Ally McBeal,

00:10:30.920 --> 00:10:32.510
but we also don't sell our souls

00:10:32.510 --> 00:10:34.153
the same way that Jeff Sessions did.

00:10:34.153 --> 00:10:38.614
♪ Just say no to the lawyer employer, no ♪

00:10:38.614 --> 00:10:41.870
♪ Don't be a lawyer ♪

00:10:41.870 --> 00:10:44.170
- What about human rights law?

00:10:44.170 --> 00:10:45.195
- Oh no.

00:10:45.195 --> 00:10:46.440
♪ No money, no, no money ♪

00:10:46.440 --> 00:10:47.450
- Oh no!

00:10:47.450 --> 00:10:49.106
- Environmental law?

00:10:49.106 --> 00:10:52.350
♪ No money, even less money ♪
(laughing)

00:10:52.350 --> 00:10:54.010
- Immigration law?

00:10:54.010 --> 00:10:56.386
♪ No money, plus it's a bummer ♪

00:10:56.386 --> 00:10:58.270
(groans)

00:10:58.270 --> 00:11:00.990
- That hurts my heart
because it's so true.

00:11:00.990 --> 00:11:02.760
There are so many people
that want to go into

00:11:02.760 --> 00:11:05.213
these different variations
of public interest law,

00:11:05.213 --> 00:11:07.743
and they really don't pay very well.

00:11:07.743 --> 00:11:11.210
Also I would lump into that,
being a public defender,

00:11:11.210 --> 00:11:13.330
which look, I am not the kind of person

00:11:13.330 --> 00:11:15.190
that rallies behind district attorneys,

00:11:15.190 --> 00:11:17.480
I think it is totally proper and good

00:11:17.480 --> 00:11:21.090
that public defenders are
out there providing a defense

00:11:21.090 --> 00:11:25.020
to the indigent, but there's so many forms

00:11:25.020 --> 00:11:27.590
of legal profession that are worthwhile

00:11:27.590 --> 00:11:28.850
and they're good for the country

00:11:28.850 --> 00:11:30.500
and they're good for the soul,

00:11:30.500 --> 00:11:33.080
but they really don't pay very much,

00:11:33.080 --> 00:11:36.460
and things like environmental
law and immigration law,

00:11:36.460 --> 00:11:38.310
yeah, they definitely
fall into that category.

00:11:38.310 --> 00:11:42.260
You know what really gets it
right is How I Met Your Mother.

00:11:42.260 --> 00:11:44.190
Marshall is a law student in that

00:11:44.190 --> 00:11:46.770
and he eventually becomes
an environmental lawyer.

00:11:46.770 --> 00:11:49.710
He leaves big law and takes a salary cut

00:11:49.710 --> 00:11:53.270
of like 100% or like 90%
of what his salary is

00:11:53.270 --> 00:11:55.100
to go into this environmental law,

00:11:55.100 --> 00:11:59.154
and that's, that's
really, really accurate.

00:11:59.154 --> 00:12:00.370
(upbeat music)
- Okay but,

00:12:00.370 --> 00:12:02.451
what if one day you make
it to the Supreme Court?

00:12:02.451 --> 00:12:05.285
♪ It'd be great to be
on the Supreme Court ♪

00:12:05.285 --> 00:12:07.803
♪ But you'll never be
on the Supreme Court ♪

00:12:07.803 --> 00:12:09.109
(laughing)

00:12:09.109 --> 00:12:11.818
♪ There's truly no chance
of that happening ♪

00:12:11.818 --> 00:12:12.920
- That's so true, I mean, think about it,

00:12:12.920 --> 00:12:14.990
there are nine justices
on the Supreme Court

00:12:14.990 --> 00:12:17.700
and there are literally
hundreds of thousands

00:12:17.700 --> 00:12:20.090
of attorneys in the US alone,

00:12:20.090 --> 00:12:23.030
not to mention hundreds of
thousands of law students,

00:12:23.030 --> 00:12:26.381
tens of thousands of law
professors out there.

00:12:26.381 --> 00:12:28.920
The chances of getting
one of the nine spots

00:12:28.920 --> 00:12:30.910
on the Supreme Court are basically zero.

00:12:30.910 --> 00:12:34.020
Plus, you need to be effectively
personal friends with

00:12:34.020 --> 00:12:36.750
the president or potentially
the Senate Majority Leader.

00:12:36.750 --> 00:12:38.760
So yeah, you're not getting
on the Supreme Court.

00:12:38.760 --> 00:12:41.410
Also, that would be a terrible job.

00:12:41.410 --> 00:12:43.680
I just don't understand why
people would want to do it.

00:12:43.680 --> 00:12:45.900
Maybe if they are power hungry I guess,

00:12:45.900 --> 00:12:48.589
but man, doesn't seem worth it to me.

00:12:48.589 --> 00:12:49.610
(upbeat music)

00:12:49.610 --> 00:12:51.640
- The preceding song in now way reflects

00:12:51.640 --> 00:12:54.563
the views of CBS and the CW network.

00:12:55.587 --> 00:12:56.943
Ah screw it.

00:12:58.087 --> 00:12:59.219
(yells)

00:12:59.219 --> 00:13:01.780
- (laughing) Oh no.

00:13:01.780 --> 00:13:04.470
I had some very interesting interactions

00:13:04.470 --> 00:13:06.680
with the lawyers from CBS.

00:13:06.680 --> 00:13:09.070
That's uh, that's a story for another day,

00:13:09.070 --> 00:13:12.540
but let's just say I'm not super sad

00:13:12.540 --> 00:13:14.766
that that just happened
to that CBS lawyer.

00:13:14.766 --> 00:13:16.650
(upbeat music)
(body thuds)

00:13:16.650 --> 00:13:17.905
- See?

00:13:17.905 --> 00:13:19.800
♪ Don't be a lawyer ♪

00:13:19.800 --> 00:13:21.090
- Oh my god.

00:13:21.090 --> 00:13:24.264
Oh it's so good, it's so good.

00:13:24.264 --> 00:13:26.931
(playful music)

00:13:28.140 --> 00:13:29.070
So with that being said,

00:13:29.070 --> 00:13:32.286
let's give Crazy Ex-Girlfriend
a grade for legal realism.

00:13:32.286 --> 00:13:35.110
(gavel bangs)

00:13:35.110 --> 00:13:38.220
On the one hand, you have
some hilarious insights

00:13:38.220 --> 00:13:41.310
into the crap that new
attorneys have to deal with,

00:13:41.310 --> 00:13:43.690
the drudgery, sometimes the lack of pay,

00:13:43.690 --> 00:13:46.010
sometimes not getting
the job that you want.

00:13:46.010 --> 00:13:49.530
There's a lot of really terrible
outcomes for a large swath

00:13:49.530 --> 00:13:51.450
of the people that are out
there that become lawyers.

00:13:51.450 --> 00:13:53.210
On the other hand, if you're like me

00:13:53.210 --> 00:13:55.490
and you were watching Law &amp; Order for fun

00:13:55.490 --> 00:13:57.840
when you were 10 years old and
convinced your parents that

00:13:57.840 --> 00:14:00.750
it was educational TV because
you wanted to be a lawyer,

00:14:00.750 --> 00:14:02.970
then law school is probably right for you,

00:14:02.970 --> 00:14:05.070
and being a lawyer is
probably right for you.

00:14:05.070 --> 00:14:08.670
I have never regretted the time
that I've spent practicing.

00:14:08.670 --> 00:14:10.900
I've been practicing
for over 12 years now,

00:14:10.900 --> 00:14:13.100
and most of it has been wonderful.

00:14:13.100 --> 00:14:15.210
Once you get past the drudgery of being

00:14:15.210 --> 00:14:17.140
a first or second or third year attorney,

00:14:17.140 --> 00:14:18.810
it gets better after that.

00:14:18.810 --> 00:14:20.410
So you know, you can
push all the grunt work

00:14:20.410 --> 00:14:24.300
onto those other poor new
first year attorneys out there,

00:14:24.300 --> 00:14:26.700
and I suspect it's the same
if you're a doctor as well.

00:14:26.700 --> 00:14:28.690
The first years are terrible
and then it gets better

00:14:28.690 --> 00:14:30.550
over time and the pay
gets better over time.

00:14:30.550 --> 00:14:32.887
So all in all, I give Crazy Ex-Girlfriend

00:14:32.887 --> 00:14:35.100
"Don't Be A Lawyer" an A minus.

00:14:35.100 --> 00:14:37.530
It's a great song and a hilarious critique

00:14:37.530 --> 00:14:38.530
of the legal profession.

